  "title": "HP-75D Portable Computer",
  "year": "1982",
  "subtitle": "A handheld BASIC computer you load with software by sliding magnetic cards beside the spacebar — then it wakes up on a schedule to run your programs.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The HP-75 inverts how software gets into a machine: instead of typing or a disk, you physically slide magnetic cards into a built-in reader beside the spacebar, snap ROM modules into ports, or (on the D) sweep a barcode wand over printed data. Its appointment scheduler doesn't just beep — it can execute BASIC programs on schedule, a real-time-control 'personal intelligence appliance' a decade before the PDA. Distinct from the museum's TI-59 (numeric calculator with external card data) and from all organizer exhibits (no card reader, no scheduled execution).",
  "search_terms": ["HP-75D", "HP-75C", "magnetic card reader", "handheld BASIC computer", "HP-75 scheduler", "HP-75 card reader", "KANGAROO", "MERLIN HP-75"],
